{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "Title: Mythbusting the Kalman Filters\n",
        "author: \"Evan Tate Paterson Hughes\"\n",
        "format:\n",
        "  html:\n",
        "    code-fold: true\n",
        "    toc: true\n",
        "    include-in-header: header.html\n",
        "    mathjax: \n",
        "      extensions: [\"breqn\", \"bm\", \"ams\"]\n",
        "bibliography: Bibliography.bib\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "There are quite a few things being said about the kalman filter, and its variants, that don't seem to hold up.\n",
        "\n",
        "This page is to cover a few of those things which, in particular, should provide some intuition as to how the variants of the filter gain their advantages (and disadvantages).\n",
        "\n",
        "We will only cover topics relating to the standard DLM case, written as \n",
        "\n",
        "\\begin{align*}\n",
        "  \\bv Z_t &= \\Phi_t \\bv Y_t + \\bv \\epsilon_t, &\\quad t = 1,\\dots, T,\\\\\n",
        "  \\bv Y_{t+1} &= M_t \\bv Y_t + \\bv \\eta_t, &\\quad t = 0,\\dots, T-1,\n",
        "\\end{align*}\n",
        "\n",
        "initialised at $\\bv Y_0 \\sim \\mathcal N(\\bv m_0, P_0)$, with $\\bv \\epsilon_t \\sim \\mathcal N(\\bv 0, \\Sigma_t^{(\\epsilon)})$, independant of eachother and of $\\bv \\eta_t \\sim \\mathcal N(\\bv 0, \\Sigma_t^{(\\eta)})$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Make sure to read up on the filters on [this page, under the Kalman Filter heading.](site/mathematics.html)\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## The Information Filter\n",
        "\n",
        "### Myth 1: You can use a truly vague prior distribution using the information filter.\n",
        "\n",
        "Likely one of the most common things said about information filter is that it allows for initialisation whith zero information.\n",
        "\n",
        "This covers one of the more initially confusing aspects of the Kalman Filter; specification of the distribution $\\bv Y_0$. \n",
        "This corresponds to the Kalman filter being a kind of Bayesian filter; that is, it requires prior knowledge on the state.\n",
        "Generally, this prior is simply put to be reasonably vague; put $\\bv m_0$ to be whatever and $P_0$ to be some large multiple of the identity matrix, unless we do actually have prior knowledge, in which case we use that.\n",
        "\n",
        "But is it possible to filter using no information at all?\n",
        "In Bayesian statistics, this would be what is know as a 'flat prior', where the prior is improper as it uses infinite initial variance (the density function is now 0 everywhere, making it not a 'proper' density since it no longer intergrates to 1).\n",
        "\n",
        "The information filter does, indeed, give an easy way to do this: set the initial information matrix to the zero matrix; which is intuitive.\n",
        "\n",
        "Spinning up a quick tracking example, "
      ],
      "id": "d237fd7e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| code-fold: true\n",
        "import jax\n",
        "import jax.numpy as jnp\n",
        "import jax.lax as jl\n",
        "import jax.random as jr\n",
        "import jax.scipy as jsc\n",
        "from jax.scipy.linalg import solve_triangular as st\n",
        "\n",
        "import plotly.graph_objects as go\n",
        "import jaxidem.plotters as plotters\n",
        "from jaxidem.plotters import cat\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "jax.config.update(\"jax_enable_x64\", True)\n",
        "\n",
        "dt = 0.1\n",
        "M = jnp.eye(4) + dt * jnp.eye(4, k=2)\n",
        "PHI = jnp.eye(2, 4)\n",
        "S2_eta = 5  * jnp.kron(jnp.array([[dt**3/3, dt**2/2],\n",
        "                                [dt**2/2, dt]]), \n",
        "                     jnp.eye(2))\n",
        "S2_eps = 0.2 ** 2 * jnp.eye(2)\n",
        "S_eta = jnp.linalg.cholesky(S2_eta)\n",
        "S_eps = jnp.linalg.cholesky(S2_eps)\n",
        "alphas = [jnp.array([0., 0., 1., -1.])]\n",
        "zs = []\n",
        "T = 50\n",
        "\n",
        "key = jr.PRNGKey(seed=1)\n",
        "\n",
        "for i in range(T):\n",
        "    inn_key = jr.fold_in(key, i)\n",
        "    proc_key, obs_key = jr.split(inn_key, 2)\n",
        "    alphas.append(M @ alphas[-1] + S_eta @ jr.normal(proc_key, shape = (4,)))\n",
        "    zs.append(PHI @ alphas[-1] + S_eps @ jr.normal(obs_key, shape = (2,)))\n",
        "alphas = jnp.array(alphas)\n",
        "zs = jnp.array(zs)\n",
        "\n",
        "@jax.jit\n",
        "def information_filter(\n",
        "        nu_0,\n",
        "        Q_0,\n",
        "        M,\n",
        "        PHI,\n",
        "        S2_eta,\n",
        "        S2_eps,\n",
        "        zs\n",
        "):\n",
        "\n",
        "    r = nu_0.shape[0]\n",
        "    nobs = zs.shape[1]\n",
        "\n",
        "    i_s = jax.vmap(lambda z: PHI.T @ jsc.linalg.solve(S2_eps, z, assume_a = \"pos\"), in_axes=0)(zs)\n",
        "    I = PHI.T @ jsc.linalg.solve(S2_eps, PHI, assume_a = \"pos\")\n",
        "    \n",
        "    Minv = jnp.linalg.inv(M)\n",
        "    S2_eta_inv = jnp.linalg.inv(S2_eta)\n",
        "\n",
        "    @jax.jit\n",
        "    def step(carry, i):\n",
        "        nu_tt, Q_tt, _, _ = carry\n",
        "\n",
        "        S_t = Minv.T @ Q_tt @ Minv\n",
        "        J_t = jsc.linalg.solve((S_t + S2_eta_inv).T, S_t.T).T\n",
        "        \n",
        "        nu_pred = (jnp.eye(r) - J_t) @ Minv.T @ nu_tt\n",
        "        Q_pred = (jnp.eye(r) - J_t) @ S_t\n",
        "\n",
        "        nu_up = nu_pred + i\n",
        "        Q_up = Q_pred + I\n",
        "\n",
        "        new_carry = (nu_up, Q_up, nu_pred, Q_pred)\n",
        "        \n",
        "        return new_carry, new_carry\n",
        "    \n",
        "    carry, seq = jl.scan(\n",
        "        step,\n",
        "        (nu_0, Q_0, jnp.zeros(r), jnp.eye(r)),\n",
        "        i_s,\n",
        "    )\n",
        "    \n",
        "    nus, Qs, nupreds, Qpreds = (seq[0], seq[1], seq[2], seq[3])\n",
        "\n",
        "    @jax.jit\n",
        "    def likelihood_func(z, nu_pred, Q_pred):\n",
        "        e = z - PHI @ jnp.linalg.solve(Q_pred, nu_pred)\n",
        "        chol_Sigma_t = jnp.linalg.cholesky(\n",
        "            PHI @ jnp.linalg.solve(Q_pred, PHI.T) + S2_eps\n",
        "        )\n",
        "        z = st(chol_Sigma_t, e, lower=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "        ll = (\n",
        "            -jnp.sum(jnp.log(jnp.diag(chol_Sigma_t)))\n",
        "            - 0.5 * jnp.dot(z, z)\n",
        "            - 0.5 * nobs * jnp.log(2 * jnp.pi)\n",
        "        )\n",
        "        return ll\n",
        "\n",
        "    lls = jax.vmap(likelihood_func)(zs, nupreds, Qpreds)\n",
        "    ll = jnp.sum(lls)\n",
        "    \n",
        "    filt_results = {\"ll\": ll,\n",
        "                    \"nus\": nus,\n",
        "                    \"Qs\": Qs,\n",
        "                    \"nupreds\": nupreds,\n",
        "                    \"Qpreds\": Qpreds}\n",
        "\n",
        "    return filt_results\n",
        "\n",
        "nu_0 = jnp.zeros(4)\n",
        "Q_0 = jnp.zeros((4,4))\n",
        "\n",
        "filt_results_inf = information_filter(nu_0, Q_0, M, PHI, S2_eta, S2_eps, zs)\n",
        "\n",
        "# exclude the first point; its nan since Q_0 is 0\n",
        "ms = jnp.linalg.solve(filt_results_inf['Qs'], filt_results_inf['nus'][..., None]).squeeze(-1)[1:]\n",
        "Ps = jnp.linalg.inv(filt_results_inf['Qs'])[1:]\n",
        "\n",
        "m_x, m_y = ms[1:50, 0], ms[1:50, 1] \n",
        "s2_x, s2_y = Ps[1:50, 0,0], Ps[1:50, 1,1]\n",
        "\n",
        "fig = go.Figure()\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(\n",
        "    x=alphas[:50, 0],\n",
        "    y=alphas[:50, 1],\n",
        "    mode='lines+markers',\n",
        "    name='Process',\n",
        "    line=dict(color=cat[\"blue\"]),\n",
        "    marker=dict(size=6)\n",
        "))\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(\n",
        "    x=zs[:50, 0],\n",
        "    y=zs[:50, 1],\n",
        "    mode='lines+markers',\n",
        "    name='Observations',\n",
        "    line=dict(color=cat[\"red\"], dash='dot', width=1),\n",
        "    marker=dict(symbol='cross', color=cat[\"red\"], size=8, line=dict(width=1))\n",
        "))\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(\n",
        "    x=m_x,\n",
        "    y=m_y,\n",
        "    mode='lines',\n",
        "    name='Filtered mean',\n",
        "    line=dict(color=cat[\"yellow\"], width=2)\n",
        "))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Uncertainty band (±1 std) for filtered position\n",
        "fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(\n",
        "    x=jnp.concatenate([m_x - jnp.sqrt(s2_x), m_x[::-1] + jnp.sqrt(s2_x[::-1])]),\n",
        "    y=jnp.concatenate([m_y - jnp.sqrt(s2_y), m_y[::-1] + jnp.sqrt(s2_y[::-1])]),\n",
        "    fill='toself',\n",
        "    fillcolor='rgba(250, 179, 135, 0.2)',\n",
        "    line=dict(color='rgba(0,0,0,0)'),\n",
        "    showlegend=True,\n",
        "    name='±1 std deviation'\n",
        "))\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.update_layout(\n",
        "    title='Tracking Model',\n",
        "    xaxis_title='X',\n",
        "    yaxis_title='Y',\n",
        "    legend=dict(x=0.02, y=0.98),\n",
        "    width=700,\n",
        "    height=500,\n",
        "    paper_bgcolor=cat[\"base\"],\n",
        "    plot_bgcolor=cat[\"base\"],\n",
        "    font=dict(color=cat[\"text\"], size=16),\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "plotters.apply_catppuccin_mocha(fig)\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.show()\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Log-Likelihood = {filt_results_inf['ll']}\")"
      ],
      "id": "f7f7c166",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "As we can see, the filtering was fine.\n",
        "Note, however that the likelihood failed; this is as expected. \n",
        "When you consider the first likelihood term, $p(\\bv z_1)$, it is clear that the likelihood is zero (therefore the log likelihood is undefined); if our prior on $\\bv Y_0$ is flat, the corresponding prior predictive distribution of $\\bv Z_1$ is also!\n",
        "You can still recover a 'partial likeihood' $p(\\bv z_t, \\dots, \\bv z_2 \\mid \\bv z_1)$, however, by simply excluding the first element of the sum of the log likelihoods at the last step of the information filter.\n",
        "\n",
        "So, myth confirmed right? \n",
        "Well, yes, but what I really want to talk about is the way in which people tend to say it; the wikipedia page, as of September 2025, reads \n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note icon=false}\n",
        "\n",
        "## ['Information Filter' from Wikipedia](en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalman_filter#Information_filter)\n",
        "\n",
        "Additionally, the information filter allows for system information initialization according to $I_{1\\mid0} = P_{1\\mid0}^{-1}=0$, which would not be possible for the regular Kalman filter.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "That last statement, 'which would not be possible for the regular Kalman filter', is not true at all.\n",
        "The standard Kalman filter can absolutely handle this case.\n",
        "\n",
        "Plugging an infinite matrix into standard implementations likely won't work; instead, you need to do the first step 'by hand'. \n",
        "The is covered in the appendix of 'The Maths' page [here.](site/mathematics.html)\n",
        "In short, the first step becomes\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\begin{split}\n",
        "  \\bv m_{1\\mid1} &= (\\Phi_1^\\intercal \\Sigma_\\epsilon^{-1} \\Phi_1)^{\\dagger} \\Phi_1.T \\Sigma_\\epsilon^{-1} \\tilde{\\bv z}_1,\\\\\n",
        "  P_{1\\mid1} &= (\\Phi_1^\\intercal \\Sigma_\\epsilon^{\\dagger} \\Phi_1)^{\\dagger},\n",
        "\\end{split}\n",
        "$$ {#eq-kalmanvague}\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\dagger$ is the generallised pseudo-inverse. \n",
        "This can be easily recognised as the correlated least-squares estimate for $Y$, so again, this makes complete sense."
      ],
      "id": "39575417"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| code-fold: true\n",
        "\n",
        "@jax.jit\n",
        "def kalman_filter_vague(M, PHI, S2_eta, S2_eps, zs):\n",
        "\n",
        "    r = M.shape[0]\n",
        "    nobs = zs.shape[1]\n",
        "\n",
        "    P1 = jnp.linalg.pinv(PHI.T@ jnp.linalg.solve(S2_eps, PHI))    \n",
        "    m1 = P1 @ PHI.T @ jnp.linalg.solve(S2_eps, zs[0])\n",
        "\n",
        "    #m1 = PHI.T @ jnp.linalg.solve(S2_eps, zs[0])\n",
        "    #P1 = PHI.T @ jnp.linalg.solve(S2_eps, PHI)\n",
        "    \n",
        "    @jax.jit\n",
        "    def step(carry, z_t):\n",
        "        m_tt, P_tt, _, _, ll = carry\n",
        "\n",
        "        # predict\n",
        "        m_pred = M @ m_tt\n",
        "        P_pred = M @ P_tt @ M.T + S2_eta\n",
        "        # Update\n",
        "        e_t = z_t - PHI @ m_pred\n",
        "        Sigma_t = PHI @ P_pred @ PHI.T + S2_eps\n",
        "        K_t = (jsc.linalg.solve(Sigma_t, PHI, assume_a = \"pos\") @ P_pred.T).T \n",
        "        \n",
        "        m_up = m_pred + K_t @ e_t\n",
        "        P_up = (jnp.eye(r) - K_t @ PHI) @ P_pred\n",
        "        \n",
        "        # Stabilised form\n",
        "        # P_up = (jnp.eye(r) - K_t @ PHI) @ P_pred @ (jnp.eye(r) - K_t @ PHI).T + K_t@S2_eps@K_t.T\n",
        "\n",
        "        # likelihood of epsilon, using cholesky decomposition\n",
        "        chol_Sigma_t = jnp.linalg.cholesky(Sigma_t)\n",
        "        z = st(chol_Sigma_t, e_t, lower=True)\n",
        "        ll_new = (\n",
        "            ll\n",
        "            - jnp.sum(jnp.log(jnp.diag(chol_Sigma_t)))\n",
        "            - 0.5 * jnp.dot(z, z)\n",
        "            - 0.5 * nobs * jnp.log(2 * jnp.pi)\n",
        "        )\n",
        "\n",
        "        new_carry = (m_up, P_up, m_pred, P_pred, ll_new)\n",
        "        return new_carry, new_carry\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "    carry, seq = jl.scan(\n",
        "        step,\n",
        "        (m1, P1, m1, P1, 0),\n",
        "        zs[1:],\n",
        "    )\n",
        "\n",
        "    ll, ms, Ps, mpreds, Ppreds = (carry[4], seq[0], seq[1], seq[2], seq[3])\n",
        "    \n",
        "    filt_results = {\"ll\": ll,\n",
        "                    \"ms\": ms,\n",
        "                    \"Ps\": Ps,\n",
        "                    \"mpreds\": mpreds,\n",
        "                    \"Ppreds\": Ppreds}\n",
        "\n",
        "    return filt_results\n",
        "\n",
        "filt_results_kal = kalman_filter_vague(M, PHI, S2_eta, S2_eps, zs)\n",
        "\n",
        "ms2 = filt_results_kal['ms']\n",
        "Ps2 = filt_results_kal['Ps']\n",
        "\n",
        "print(m_x[:])\n",
        "m_x, m_y = ms2[:, 0], ms2[:, 1]\n",
        "print(m_x[:3])\n",
        "s2_x, s2_y = Ps2[:, 0,0], Ps2[:, 1,1]\n",
        "\n",
        "fig = go.Figure()\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(\n",
        "    x=alphas[:50, 0],\n",
        "    y=alphas[:50, 1],\n",
        "    mode='lines+markers',\n",
        "    name='Process',\n",
        "    line=dict(color=cat[\"blue\"]),\n",
        "    marker=dict(size=6)\n",
        "))\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(\n",
        "    x=zs[:50, 0],\n",
        "    y=zs[:50, 1],\n",
        "    mode='lines+markers',\n",
        "    name='Observations',\n",
        "    line=dict(color=cat[\"red\"], dash='dot', width=1),\n",
        "    marker=dict(symbol='cross', color=cat[\"red\"], size=8, line=dict(width=1))\n",
        "))\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(\n",
        "    x=m_x,\n",
        "    y=m_y,\n",
        "    mode='lines',\n",
        "    name='Filtered mean',\n",
        "    line=dict(color=cat[\"yellow\"], width=2)\n",
        "))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Uncertainty band (±1 std) for filtered position\n",
        "fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(\n",
        "    x=jnp.concatenate([m_x - jnp.sqrt(s2_x), m_x[::-1] + jnp.sqrt(s2_x[::-1])]),\n",
        "    y=jnp.concatenate([m_y - jnp.sqrt(s2_y), m_y[::-1] + jnp.sqrt(s2_y[::-1])]),\n",
        "    fill='toself',\n",
        "    fillcolor='rgba(250, 179, 135, 0.2)',\n",
        "    line=dict(color='rgba(0,0,0,0)'),\n",
        "    showlegend=True,\n",
        "    name='±1 std deviation'\n",
        "))\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.update_layout(\n",
        "    title='Tracking Model',\n",
        "    xaxis_title='X',\n",
        "    yaxis_title='Y',\n",
        "    legend=dict(x=0.02, y=0.98),\n",
        "    width=700,\n",
        "    height=500,\n",
        "    paper_bgcolor=cat[\"base\"],\n",
        "    plot_bgcolor=cat[\"base\"],\n",
        "    font=dict(color=cat[\"text\"], size=16),\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "plotters.apply_catppuccin_mocha(fig)\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.show()"
      ],
      "id": "84dd12f2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "To check that the results are the same as with the information filter,"
      ],
      "id": "eb9bcf99"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "print(jnp.allclose(ms, ms2))\n",
        "print(ms[:3])\n",
        "print(ms2[:3])"
      ],
      "id": "d5aa84aa",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    }
